    array.push(cH, "Paris stayed silent all day, and the dollar walks into the Fed minutes still one notch heavier.|UK inflation hit 3.6%, right on the poll, and that was not enough to give cable a lift.|Another day parked under 160, and Tokyo's officials let the level speak for itself.|The franc's alarm from Tuesday is still ringing, even though the model will not listen until Friday.|Brent sagged toward $92, close to its weakest since late August, and the loonie got no help from it.|The Aussie spent the day digesting its wage beat, and the tipped stand-off stayed tipped.|Wellington had nothing to say today, and the kiwi's fundamentals did not move an inch.|The pound's inflation test came back merely in line, and that takes some shine off the bearish lean.|The thinnest possible lean survived another quiet session on both ends.|A tie at −3 apiece, with the franc's paper trigger waiting for Friday's verdict.|The Aussie's wage surprise is still the freshest fact in this matchup.|No news from Paris, no news from Wellington, and the four-point lean simply carried on.|Cheaper oil nudges against the loonie, but not enough to break this stalemate.|The pound's CPI test passed without fireworks, so the stand-off goes on.|Both legs gave ground on paper today: the pound's inflation was tame and the franc's trigger still stands.|Australia's wage beat still outweighs Britain's middling inflation print.|Britain had its inflation day, New Zealand had a blank one, and the tie held.|One of the two routes to a bigger lean narrowed this morning; the oil route stays open.|The carry cross still feels Tuesday's fear spike, even with fresh wage strength behind the Aussie.|The high-yielder versus the haven, and the haven's trigger is still lit.|Across the Tasman the score is still +3 against +1, one point short of a call.|Wages up in Australia, oil down for Canada: this commodity cross kept its tilt.|Two quiet currencies, one point apart, and still no reason to pick a side.|The kiwi had nothing to say, and the franc's comeback condition from Tuesday is still met.|Oil kept sliding, and that is the side of the story that matters for this cross today.|Cheaper oil and a silent Tokyo kept both legs right where they were.|The franc's trigger sits on the table; this tie waits for Friday to decide if it counts.|A haven duel where the weaker haven's rescue condition was met on Tuesday and has not been cancelled.|Gold slipped under $3,800 today, yet its gap is decided by inputs, and none of them changed.")
    array.push(cC, "CONFIRMED (strengthened)~No new Fed remark verified today; December 25bp 96%. Shadow R3 +2, raw 6.3 → +6. FOMC minutes 21:00 Bucharest.|CONFIRMED~No verified Senate PLFSS result and no OAT–Bund reading after 155.0bp (16 Nov).|WEAKENED (watch)~UK CPI 3.6% (in line, BoE saw 3.7%), services 3.7% vs 3.8%; Dec hike odds 74%. Bearish condition half met; +1 held.|CONFIRMED (watch)~No verified MoF/BoJ comment today; raw −0.9 held at 0. Japan CPI Fri 01:30 Bucharest.|INVALIDATED (bullish condition met, mode~Tuesday's VIX close 25.46 still meets the 'VIX above 25' condition; regime re-measured only from Friday closes.|CONFIRMED (bearish reinforced)~Brent $92, near its lowest since 31 Aug; no Canadian release. Raw −4.5 held at −4.|WEAKENED (watch)~No new item after the Q3 wage beat (raw 3.6, held +3); October jobs Thu 02:30 Bucharest.|CONFIRMED~No NZ data, RBNZ or election item today.|CONFIRMED · fragile (gap −3)~G −3 unchanged; XAU shadow = G + USD shadow = +3. Gold below $3,800 is price context only.")
